  Findings: This is a solitary, well-circumscribed pigmented lesion located on the trunk. Morphologically, it presents as a papule, characterized by a raised, discrete elevation above the surrounding skin surface. The lesion exhibits a complex, heterogeneous pigmentation pattern, predominantly dark brown to black in the central and peripheral regions, with a lighter, tan-to-beige central area that appears to contain a cluster of small, irregularly shaped, whitish or hypopigmented structures, suggestive of keratinous debris or a central regression phenomenon. The borders are irregular and notched, with a scalloped appearance, and are not sharply demarcated from the surrounding skin, which appears to fade gradually into the adjacent epidermis. The surface texture is rough and granular, with visible scaling and a slightly raised, verrucous contour. There is no evidence of ulceration, crusting, or telangiectasia. The lesion lacks symmetry and does not display a radial or geometric pattern; instead, it shows an asymmetric, irregular internal structure with scattered, variably sized pigment globules and a central area of apparent regression. The overall appearance is consistent with a benign nevus, possibly a compound or dysplastic nevus, but the irregular borders and central regression features warrant careful clinical follow-up.
