        [Clinical Task]: Evidence-Based Dermatological Diagnosis.
        You are acting as a Senior Dermatologist. Your goal is to provide a final diagnosis by cross-referencing visual observations with medical standards and historical precedents, without relying on any pre-calculated model probabilities.

        [1. Current Visual Findings (from Vision Agent)]:
        This is a solitary, well-circumscribed nodule located on the dorsal aspect of the wrist. The lesion measures approximately 1.5 cm in diameter and presents with a central ulcerated crater exhibiting a yellowish-brown exudate, suggestive of chronic inflammation or infection. The surrounding tissue is erythematous and appears slightly edematous, with a distinct, raised, hyperpigmented border that transitions sharply from the lesion to the adjacent normal skin. The surface of the ulcer is irregular and appears moist, with no visible scaling, crusting, or telangiectasia. The lesion is asymmetric and lacks any discernible internal structural patterns such as a pigment network or globules. The overall morphology is consistent with a chronic, possibly infected or granulomatous nodule with secondary ulceration.

        [2. Diagnostic Prototypes (Standard Reference)]:
        - [eccrine-poroma Standard]: Eccrine poroma is a rare, benign skin tumor characterized by solitary, well-circumscribed erythematous papules on the plantar surface of the foot. These papules typically measure around 5mm in diameter, exhibiting a central area of crusting and superficial ulceration, surrounded by an erythematous halo. The borders are irregularly elevated and slightly elevated, with a distinct demarcation from the surrounding skin. Eccrine poroma lacks visible scaling, lichenification, or telangiectasia. Asymmetric and lacking discernible internal structural patterns such as pigment networks or globules, this distinctive feature distinguishes eccrine poroma from other skin tumors. Irregular small, superficial vascular lesions might mimic eccrine poroma, but they differ in size, shape, and distribution pattern. Cherry angiomas or other benign vascular proliferations could potentially resemble these lesions, but they lack the unique characteristics of eccrine poroma.
- [pyogenic-granuloma Standard]: Pyogenic granulomas are solitary, dome-shaped papules or nodules exhibiting distinct visual features. These lesions typically present as erythematous-red, glossy, translucent papules or nodules with sharp, regular borders. The surface is smooth, taut, and free from scaling, crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia. Central, irregularly shaped, dark brown to black, hyperpigmented cores surrounded by an erythematous halo with a raised border may be observed in some cases. Satellite lesions or post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in the form of scattered dark brown macules around the periphery might also be seen. Absence of visible pigment network or radial symmetry distinguishes pyogenic granulomas from other conditions. Asymmetric presentations and absence of systemic involvement or widespread dermatoses support this distinction. Lesions may occur on various body sites including the feet, legs, face, abdomen, and elsewhere depending on their location.
- [hyperpigmentation Standard]: Hyperpigmentation refers to an increase in the amount of pigmentation in the skin, resulting in darkened patches or spots. There are various types of hyperpigmentation, including melasma, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, and nevi. The diagnostic process involves evaluating the patient's medical history, physical examination, and sometimes additional tests like skin biopsy or dermoscopy. In recent verified cases, hyperpigmentation has been diagnosed based on specific visual features such as solitary lesions, well-demarcated borders, homogeneous pigmentation, absence of pigment networks, globules, or regression areas, smooth surfaces, and lack of associated symptoms like scaling, crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia. These features help distinguish hyperpigmentation from other conditions, enabling accurate diagnosis and appropriate treatment planning.
- [kaposi-sarcoma Standard]: Kaposi sarcoma typically presents as a solitary, well-circumscribed lesion on the skin. Lesions can be pigmented or erythematous, depending on the subtype. Pigmented lesions exhibit a uniform, dark brown to near-black pigmentation, with sharp borders and a smooth surface. Erythematous lesions have a central, dome-shaped nodule with an exudative surface and a zone of hyperpigmentation. These features lack internal structural patterns and do not show signs of inflammation, ulceration, atrophy, or telangiectasia. Surrounding skin may display mild diffuse hyperpigmentation and benign lentigines. Overall, these features suggest a benign or inflammatory condition, making kaposi sarcoma a rare and notable diagnosis.
- [verruca-vulgaris Standard]: Verruca vulgaris is a skin lesion characterized by diverse morphologies, including papules, nodules, and macules. Common features include well-circumscribed, smooth, non-keratotic surfaces, varying colors from erythematous to hyperkeratotic, absence of discernible internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression scars. Lesions may exhibit signs of inflammation, infection, or significant inflammatory responses in surrounding skin. Ink markings may be present but are not considered part of the intrinsic pathology. Verruca vulgaris typically occurs alone and is not associated with surrounding erythema or inflammation.


