        [2. Diagnostic Prototypes (Standard Reference)]:
        - [basal-cell-carcinoma Standard]: Basal cell carcinomas exhibit a diverse array of morphologies, ranging from solitary, well-circumscribed lesions to those with irregular borders, notches, fading into surrounding skin, and potential ulcerations, infections, or crusting. Lesions often display unique coloration, including homogeneous pigmentation, irregular erythema, central ulceration or crusting, and varied shades of red, brown, and yellow. Additionally, they may have internal structural patterns such as dermoscopic structures like whorls, rosettes, and nests. Dermoscopy may reveal various structures like notches, irregularities, and asymmetry. The overall appearance varies depending on the progression of the disease, with some cases resembling benign conditions like seborrheic keratoses or xeroderma pigmentosum. Proper clinical examination, potentially using dermoscopy, and ancillary tests are necessary for accurate differentiation between BCC and other skin conditions.
- [pyogenic-granuloma Standard]: Pyogenic granulomas are solitary, dome-shaped papules or nodules exhibiting distinct visual features. These lesions typically present as erythematous-red, glossy, translucent papules or nodules with sharp, regular borders. The surface is smooth, taut, and free from scaling, crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia. Central, irregularly shaped, dark brown to black, hyperpigmented cores surrounded by an erythematous halo with a raised border may be observed in some cases. Satellite lesions or post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in the form of scattered dark brown macules around the periphery might also be seen. Absence of visible pigment network or radial symmetry distinguishes pyogenic granulomas from other conditions. Asymmetric presentations and absence of systemic involvement or widespread dermatoses support this distinction. Lesions may occur on various body sites including the feet, legs, face, abdomen, and elsewhere depending on their location.
- [melanocytic-nevi Standard]: Melanocytic nevi can be accurately diagnosed through their diverse range of characteristic features. These skin lesions often exhibit a smooth surface with a uniform coloration. Some nevi may display rough, scaly, or bumpy surfaces, while others might have lightly grouped collections of pigmented cells or globules. Internally, these lesions may show loosely grouped groups of pigmented cells or globules, although radial symmetry is usually absent. When evaluating melanocytic nevi, healthcare professionals should focus on identifying consistent patterns such as symmetrical shapes, homogeneous colors, and the absence of discernible internal structures to differentiate them from potential malignant lesions. A patient's history and the propensity for change must also be considered for efficient diagnosis and management.
- [kaposi-sarcoma Standard]: Kaposi sarcoma typically presents as a solitary, well-circumscribed lesion on the skin. Lesions can be pigmented or erythematous, depending on the subtype. Pigmented lesions exhibit a uniform, dark brown to near-black pigmentation, with sharp borders and a smooth surface. Erythematous lesions have a central, dome-shaped nodule with an exudative surface and a zone of hyperpigmentation. These features lack internal structural patterns and do not show signs of inflammation, ulceration, atrophy, or telangiectasia. Surrounding skin may display mild diffuse hyperpigmentation and benign lentigines. Overall, these features suggest a benign or inflammatory condition, making kaposi sarcoma a rare and notable diagnosis.
- [epidermal-cyst Standard]: Epidermal cysts typically present as solitary, small, erythematous papules or macules without central pigmentation or hypopigmentation. These lesions exhibit well-defined borders, a uniform erythematous hue, and a smooth, non-scaly, non-crusted, and non-ulcerated surface. They may be symmetrically positioned relative to their site of origin and lack internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression scars. Surrounding skin may show mild to moderate skin aging, including visible dermal furrows and scattered, faint erythematous macules, without any other concurrent lesions. In summary, these changes are considered benign, non-invasive, and represent localized atrophic or post-inflammatory hypopigmentations.


